What you need to set up

An occluder “segment” list per light
This shader does not raycast a tilemap. It renders soft shadow volumes extruded from line segments (walls/edges). You must provide a set of 2D segments (endpoint A, endpoint B) that represent the silhouettes that block the light.

A custom vertex format + mesh builder
Each segment becomes a quad (two triangles) with 4 vertices using:

Segment = (b.x, b.y, a.x, a.y) (same for all 4 verts of that segment)

ShadowCoord = (x, y) per vertex:

(0,0), (1,0), (0,1), (1,1)

Render shadow mask into a RenderTarget2D (one per light or accumulated)
Draw all segment-quads with this effect into a “shadow mask” RT using appropriate blend state.

Apply the shadow mask when drawing the scene
Typical is multiplicative darkening: finalColor *= (1 - shadowMask) or equivalent, depending on how you encode the mask.

1) Effect parameters you must set

Your effect expects:

u_matrix (float4x4): transforms from the same 2D space as your segment endpoints/light position into clip space.

u_light (float3): (lightX, lightY, lightRadius)

LightPenetration (float): small > 0 value.

In MonoGame:

effect.Parameters["u_matrix"].SetValue(matrix);
effect.Parameters["u_light"].SetValue(new Vector3(lightX, lightY, lightRadius));
effect.Parameters["LightPenetration"].SetValue(lightPenetration);
Coordinate space rule (most important)

endpoint_a, endpoint_b, and u_light.xy must be in the same space.
Pick one and stick to it:

World space (recommended), or

Screen space, or

Light-local space (harder unless you build that pipeline)

Then u_matrix must map that same space into clip space for the target you are rendering to.

2) MonoGame vertex type you need

Create a custom vertex struct matching the shader semantics:

using Microsoft.Xna.Framework;
using Microsoft.Xna.Framework.Graphics;
using System.Runtime.InteropServices;

[StructLayout(LayoutKind.Sequential)]
public struct ShadowVertex : IVertexType
{
    public Vector4 Segment;     // POSITION0
    public Vector2 ShadowCoord; // TEXCOORD0

    public static readonly VertexDeclaration VertexDeclaration = new VertexDeclaration(
        new VertexElement(0,  VertexElementFormat.Vector4, VertexElementUsage.Position, 0),
        new VertexElement(16, VertexElementFormat.Vector2, VertexElementUsage.TextureCoordinate, 0)
    );

    VertexDeclaration IVertexType.VertexDeclaration => VertexDeclaration;

    public ShadowVertex(Vector4 segment, Vector2 shadowCoord)
    {
        Segment = segment;
        ShadowCoord = shadowCoord;
    }
}
3) Building the quad per segment

For each occluder segment with endpoints a and b (Vector2), create 4 verts and 6 indices:

Vertex layout (as your comment describes):

(a, (0,0))

(b, (1,0))

(a, (0,1))

(b, (1,1))

Shader wants Segment = (b.x, b.y, a.x, a.y).

static void AddSegment(
    Vector2 a, Vector2 b,
    List<ShadowVertex> verts,
    List<ushort> indices)
{
    var seg = new Vector4(b.X, b.Y, a.X, a.Y);

    ushort baseIndex = (ushort)verts.Count;

    verts.Add(new ShadowVertex(seg, new Vector2(0f, 0f)));
    verts.Add(new ShadowVertex(seg, new Vector2(1f, 0f)));
    verts.Add(new ShadowVertex(seg, new Vector2(0f, 1f)));
    verts.Add(new ShadowVertex(seg, new Vector2(1f, 1f)));

    // Two triangles: (0,1,2) and (2,1,3)
    indices.Add((ushort)(baseIndex + 0));
    indices.Add((ushort)(baseIndex + 1));
    indices.Add((ushort)(baseIndex + 2));

    indices.Add((ushort)(baseIndex + 2));
    indices.Add((ushort)(baseIndex + 1));
    indices.Add((ushort)(baseIndex + 3));
}

Important: The segment direction (A→B vs B→A) matters for the shader’s “forward shadow clipping” (Edges.z). If you see some segments casting “inverted” shadows, ensure your segments are oriented consistently (commonly: clockwise boundary edges for closed shapes, or consistently outward-facing normals).

4) RenderTarget + blend state setup
A) Rendering the shadow mask

The shader outputs shadow in RGB with alpha = 1. You have two common choices:

Option 1: “Max” accumulate (keep darkest / largest shadow)

You want the final mask to represent strongest occlusion from any segment.

MonoGame doesn’t have a built-in MAX blend state in XNA-style BlendState, so you usually do:

render per-light to its own RT, or

encode differently and combine with custom combine pass.

Option 2: Additive accumulate (often acceptable for many segments)

Use additive blending into a mask, then clamp when applying.

This can over-darken where shadows overlap, but is simple.

A typical starting point:

Clear RT to 0 (no shadow)

BlendState = Additive (RGB add)

Later clamp in the apply step: shadow = saturate(shadow)

MonoGame:

graphicsDevice.SetRenderTarget(shadowRt);
graphicsDevice.Clear(Color.Black); // 0 shadow

graphicsDevice.BlendState = BlendState.Additive;
graphicsDevice.DepthStencilState = DepthStencilState.None;
graphicsDevice.RasterizerState = RasterizerState.CullNone;

effect.CurrentTechnique = effect.Techniques["SoftShadow"];
// set u_matrix, u_light, LightPenetration

foreach (var pass in effect.CurrentTechnique.Passes)
{
    pass.Apply();
    graphicsDevice.DrawUserIndexedPrimitives(
        PrimitiveType.TriangleList,
        verticesArray, 0, vertexCount,
        indicesArray, 0, indexCount / 3);
}
B) Applying the shadow mask to your scene

Typical: sample shadow mask and darken scene.

If the shadow RT stores “shadow amount” in [0..1], then:

scene.rgb *= (1 - shadow)

This is easiest as a separate fullscreen pass (SpriteBatch with an effect) or integrated into your tile shader.

5) Getting segment lists from a tilemap

You need segments that represent boundaries between solid and empty tiles.

The robust approach:

For each solid tile, consider its 4 edges.

If the neighbor in that direction is empty, that edge is a boundary -> add a segment for that edge.

Optionally merge collinear adjacent edges into longer segments (reduces vertex count significantly).

This is the main CPU-side work for this approach.

6) Common failure modes with this shader

Matrix/space mismatch
Endpoints are in world, but u_matrix is a screen-space transform, or vice versa.

Wrong segment orientation
Some edges “shadow forward.” Flip A/B for those edges or enforce consistent winding.

Radius/units mismatch
u_light.z must be in the same units as endpoints/light position (world units if endpoints are world).

Accumulation looks too dark
If using additive blending, overlapping can exceed 1. Clamp when applying, or use a different accumulation strategy.

